          "guid": "mscpynuxgmlweh8q",
          "message": {
            "text": "Two copies of the original exist by default; this keeps the earlier one and drops the later one, which is the opposite of what the call order suggests.\n\nbackupDataDir copies the directory before the old engine opens it. swapIntoPlace would separately retain the displaced original as .old-<ts> after the migration. Keeping both leaves two full-size duplicates after every upgrade — on the maintainer's own 35 MB database that is 70 MB of redundancy.\n\nThe pre-open copy is the better one to keep. Starting a Postgres cluster is not a read-only act: WAL replay and crash recovery can write to the directory during open. So the .old-<ts> copy is post-open state, while the backup is provably what the user had. Hence keep: 1 on the backup and keepOld: false on the swap.\n\nkeepOld: false is safe because the library removes the displaced directory only after the new cluster is successfully renamed into place — a failed swap restores the original instead.\n\nSee src/db/connection.ts for why a failure here must not be treated as corruption.\n",
            "markdown": "Two copies of the original exist by default; this keeps the earlier one and drops the later one, which is the opposite of what the call order suggests.\n\n`backupDataDir` copies the directory *before* the old engine opens it. `swapIntoPlace` would separately retain the displaced original as `.old-<ts>` after the migration. Keeping both leaves two full-size duplicates after every upgrade — on the maintainer's own 35 MB database that is 70 MB of redundancy.\n\nThe pre-open copy is the better one to keep. Starting a Postgres cluster is not a read-only act: WAL replay and crash recovery can write to the directory during open. So the `.old-<ts>` copy is post-open state, while the backup is provably what the user had. Hence `keep: 1` on the backup and `keepOld: false` on the swap.\n\n`keepOld: false` is safe because the library removes the displaced directory only after the new cluster is successfully renamed into place — a failed swap restores the original instead.\n\nSee `src/db/connection.ts` for why a failure here must not be treated as corruption.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/db/migrate-major.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 125,
                  "endLine": 158,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    // Taken before the old engine opens the directory. That ordering is the\n    // point: starting a cluster can write to it (WAL replay, recovery), so this\n    // is the only copy guaranteed to be exactly what the user had. It also\n    // makes the swap's own `.old-<ts>` copy redundant — see `keepOld` below.\n    const backupPath = await backupDataDir(dbPath, { keep: 1 });\n\n    // The old engine must be named explicitly: `openDataDir` prefers an\n    // installed engine, and the one installed here is the *new* major that\n    // cannot read this directory.\n    const source = await openDataDir(dbPath, engine.entry, { pgliteOptions: DB_OPTIONS });\n    // The staged cluster already carries the schema from `buildTarget`; this\n    // only reopens it.\n    const target = new PGlite(staged, DB_OPTIONS);\n    try {\n      await target.waitReady;\n\n      const report = await migrate({ source, target });\n      const schema = await introspectSchema(source);\n      const validation = await validateMigration(source, target, schema, 'full');\n      if (!validation.ok) {\n        throw new Error(`post-migration validation failed: ${summarize(validation)}`);\n      }\n      const rows = report.tables.reduce((n, t) => n + t.rowsCopied, 0);\n\n      // Both clusters must be closed before the swap: the directories are being\n      // renamed out from under whatever still holds them open.\n      await source.close();\n      await target.close();\n      // `keepOld: false` because the pre-open backup above already preserves the\n      // original, and it is the better copy of the two. Retaining both would\n      // leave two full-size duplicates of the database on disk after every\n      // upgrade. The displaced directory is only removed once the new cluster is\n      // successfully in place.\n      await swapIntoPlace(dbPath, staged, { keepOld: false });"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "tags": [
              "rationale"
            ]
          },
          "relatedLocations": [
            {
              "id": 0,
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/db/connection.ts"
                },
